en you're there and you get your blood  taken this is the company creating the  devices that give you those rapid  results  they have sensors in them that relay  quantitative metrics and help diagnose  instrument failures but what this  company was doing was deploying the  hardware in the field and finding up  retroactively that there was a problem  so they would take the machine back and  analyze all of the data coming off the  machines  this seemed like a perfect opportunity  to use anomaly detection we wanted to  identify those problems before they went  out into the field  so here's what the data and  experimentation looks like we have  various metrics coming off these  machines they're things like protein  levels and analytes and they come off at  different aggregations 24 hours 12 hours  Etc  this anomaly detection function has a  bunch of parameters and we just play it  around with some of them and iterated on  things like what percentage of the data  should we capture anomalies for uh  should it be positive and negative  directions so above or below the  expected threshold the level of  significance maybe we really need to be  stringent here maybe we can you know  only go with 90 confidence versus 99  percent  and then that piecewise median period  that I mentioned  another thing we realized was there are  some outliers that are just insane and  anomaly detection would always pick up  and that's very indicative of a hardware  failure but the client already knew  about these they said well we know that  these are problematic so we actually did  some outlier removal and then ran the  anomaly detection and of course then  there were no anomalies detected the  input data very much makes a difference  on your end results  so those are all parameters we wanted to  play with and we were literally manually  analyzing all of this and it turns out  uh putting it into a shiny app and just  allowing myself and the client to play  with these different parameters we  quickly got feedback on hey this  actually looks like a good threshold to  use these are good parameters let's run  with these  from there we thought well instead of a  sort of top-down approach and exploring  all the different options let's alert  the user and exactly where to go so we  took the high significance anomalies  highlighted them and allowed the user to  then click one so I want to know more  about this one what's going on with  potassium or calcium and it would bring  you right back to that screen  pre-populate all the parameters again  just a nice little user experience thing  Time Saver and they could dig into the  details a little more  the code was pretty straightforward the  input data looks just like you'd expect  for a Time series there's a time stamp  and a measure we iterated over the  different significance values we also  defaulted to using one year of data  prior to the date range that the client  is actually interested in so if I care  about May of 2020 let's start with data  from May 1st of 2019 to get some of that  seasonality and more data is better for  this type of algorithm  but throughout the project uh they then  informed us hey we actually have these  limits that we we already know it's like  a you can consider it a prior Invasion  Map and so  upper limit lower limit is something  that they would say these are  automatically bad so we thought well  this seems like it's guiding us a little  more and we could probably integrate  this first we start with a completely  data driven approach agnostic from any  business logic uh but then we said well  maybe we can incorporate these these  limits and even giving them a simple  tool like this to explore those  different outliers was something that  they didn't have and they were really  doing this all pretty manually and by  hand  so then we combine those rules with the  anomaly detection and this is over a  course of a few months we got better and  better we were finding these parameters  so now the colors you see here the green  is just an anomaly that the algorithm  found based on the parameters we already  tweaked  the blue means it's approaching that  threshold limit  and these lines these vertical lines are  those thresholds which vary by analyte  and then the red is both so we decided  these are probably most important to  look at because there's strong signal  here not only your approaching limit but  it's the anomaly detection is saying  this is  you know trending differently based on  this piecewise median  the other thing we did was allow the  user to specify how stringent they  wanted this approaching limit to be so  here you can see it's not that close to  the line we were able to Define  thresholds like maybe they really want  it to get close and the way we did that  was based on quartiles so the closer  they were to the quartile of that range  the top quartile that was more stringent  and so on and we let them kind of open  up the lens so to speak to be looser  more stringent  this ended up being the best approach uh  for the client Just Not only was it the  algorithm but incorporating the business  logic as well  so when I jump quickly into the next  examples that we have time for questions  the other example and I should wrap up  also that they're using this on a  day-to-day basis it runs as a shiny app  they're able to it connects live to  their database and it's been used as a  tool to basically direct their  investigation and identify these  Hardware failures before the machines  get shipped out to the hospital so we  thought we think that's a really cool  application  and it's it's R and shiny um the price  tag is pretty good  so real world example again is a another  client that works in the sports and  entertainment business they actually do  market research around Sports technology  so things like in venue betting and  understanding how to engage fans in this  new technical age uh biometric sensors  on athletes you name it basically any  sports technology  and I like to call them kind of a market  research company for sports Tech  so what they have is a whole bunch of  news articles that they collect via  Feedly diff bot these are different  third-party tools and Leo is something  that's I believe part of Feedly and it  analyzes all the information for you  it's kind of like a  smart filter to bring in the information  they care about  okay so the data is thousands of sports  news articles  there's also entity tagging that this  Leo tool does at TAG sports teams  Technologies vendors meaning vendors  like Google Microsoft Nike  Technologies being anything from  blockchain to augmented reality  and so what we've thought here was they  wanted interesting news stories to be  surfaced to them  so we simply took the number of mentions  of these various entities over times you  could imagine Boston Red Sox are  mentioned 10 times a week suddenly  they're mentioned 30 let's investigate  why  so here's what the first iteration looks  like we have a date range we have they  can filter by league if they'd like  we're looking at all leaks here and we  have a list of anomalies that our  package bubbled up to the top  the Seattle Sounders here were  detected we click that and it gives us a  Time series look at that particular  anomaly  we can then click it and discover more  about what's happening  so on that date and shiny pops up this  nice little module  uh  the Seattle Sounders and Amazon work  together Amazon had acquired streaming  rights for Prime video soccer matches  and that's a huge thing that this  company would like to know about  so from there you can see how you can  rapid prototype and it might be manual  and your surface some anomalies but you  could imagine how this could be  automated into report give me the five  most interesting stories that week give  me the teams that are spiking in the  news and so on  another cool use case for them was it we  used it it doubled as a QC tool so some  certain things would be spiking that  shouldn't be for example the Ottawa  Senators they weren't doing particularly  well there there was really no reason it  wasn't like the playoffs or anything  this is an NHL team so we decided to dig  into it  and it turned out that diffbot was  tagging  Ott for the Ottawa Senators because  that's our abbreviation  but it also stands for over-the-top  streaming services  so we had identified a problem with the  tagging we alerted dipbot and we got  that all fixed you know on the on the  data itself so it can also be used as a  data QC tool not only to surface  interesting things but potentially  problematic things as we talked about  also in the first example of finding  Hardware failures  and now I'm leaving I think at least  four minutes for questions but final  thoughts  um again working with people is the  hardest thing we're lucky that uh  usually they're  people we've worked with so far  understand the problem and they're very  uh collaborative and I think being able  to show them these results piecemeal is  what builds up the confidence and their  understanding of the tool and why it  matters giving them the why or allowing  them to dig in to the why and Explore  More on their own is that sort of  self-service and and  freedom of analysis that they need to to  really feel like they're engaged so  Chinese been just incredible for that  because I remember the days where you  just had to show them static charts and  put together a PowerPoint and it's very  much like I'm just talking to you rather  than working together  so anomaly detection is a useful  data-driven tool when used appropriately  in our cases we had identified specific  needs for each scenario and tailored  them accordingly uh the first use case  was for proactively identifying Hardware  failures we learned over time that  incorporating their threshold limits  would actually give us more accurate  results rather than just relying on the  data uh and the other use case surface  interesting news stories as well as  doubled as a data QC tool  work always work closely with your end  users via that iterative process shiny  again is fantastic for this  I always say build fast and fail quickly  I'd rather show you something that you  think is terrible and you tear it apart  and getting that constructive criticism  to me is a good sign it means you're  invested it means you have ideas and it  means that we can we can improve upon it  very fast  and then don't leave out important  business logic where it can help you we  don't always have to just rely on  statistical models and assume it's going  to solve all our problems there's often  times that we can incorporate that  domain knowledge and it can really  improve our results so always be looking  for those uh pieces of logic that can  help your your automation or your math  or your your art package  and questions feel free again to contact  me here I look forward to hopefully  being there next year and uh enjoy the  rest of the conference  [Applause]  [Music]

